  "query_preview": "/MONDAY NIGHT THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 258 PM CDT Sat Sep 26 2026\n\nThe dry weather is short lived, as Tuesday looks like the first of \nseveral more rainy days with renewed flooding potential. By Tuesday, \nthe western trough is cutoff from the main flow and slowly makes its \nway towards Iowa, leaving the area sitting beneath several days of \nsouthwest flow. Particularly on Tuesday and Wednesday and perhaps \nThursday, a slow moving surface boundary looks to set up SW to NE \nover Iowa with repeated flare ups of the LLJ and a persistent \nmoisture plume with PWATs of 1.5-2\". The wild card in all this is \nthe behavior of Hurricane Polo, whose remnants at mid levels may \nmake it past the Sierra Madre mountains in Mexico and help to \nenhance the gulf moisture transport ahead of the cutoff low. The \nresult is high confidence in repeated rounds of heavy rainfall and a \nfew thunderstorms mid week. WPC QPF guidance shows widespread 48hr \ntotals near 2-4\", and the threat for localized higher totals due to \ntraining thunderstorms is definitely present. Given already high \nsoil moisture content, expected totals will be enough to bring up \nflash flood and river flood threats. Experimental HEFS guidance has \na 50% chance that many central and southern Iowa rivers and streams \nreach or exceed action stage and border on minor flood stage next \nweek, and a 30% chance that some (Raccoon/Skunk/Thompson) reach \nminor to moderate. With so many moving pieces on the synoptic and \nmesoscale, trends will need to be monitored and changes are\nexpected.",
